      {
        "heading": "Active Suppression Evidence",
        "body": "A major alternative is that alpha increases simply mark return to baseline idling while attended regions remain desynchronized. The bilateral rapid-serial presentation design challenges that view because stimulation continues in both hemifields and alpha rises above pre-cue baseline over the ignored stream, making active suppression the stronger interpretation.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Perceptual Consequences",
        "body": "The review connects alpha to behavior by describing correlations between higher pre-stimulus alpha and worse near-threshold detection or discrimination. The causal case is strengthened by rhythmic TMS: 10 Hz stimulation, but not 5 Hz or 20 Hz stimulation, changes visual detection in a spatially specific pattern consistent with alpha-band suppression.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
